   Environmental Education, SAN FRANCISCO BAY AREA, CA, NOV 01-05, 1996},
Organization = {N Amer Assoc Environm Educ},
Abstract = {TTe Kentucky Institute for the Environment and Sustainable Development,
   sponsored by the University of Louisville and its current 8 centers,
   {[}Center for Environmental Education (CEEd.), Center for Environmental
   Engineering (CEEng.), Center for Environmental Health Sciences (CEHS),
   Center for Environmental Management (CEM), Center for Environmental
   Policy (CEP), Center for Environmental Sciences (CES) and Center for
   Sustainable Urban Communities (CSUC)], has been in existence for four
   years. Faculty in the various centers work together to conduct research,
   write grant proposals, develop individual course outlines, present
   monthly seminar speakers, and do community outreach. Recently, the
   institute has begun to develop environmental studies strands within an
   existing Interdisciplinary Master of Science Degree housed in the
   Graduate School. The degree has as a foundation two common core courses
   which the students take at the beginning of the program.
   (1)Environmental Science, Engineering and Medicine and (2) Environmental
   Education, Law, Management and Policy. Team taught by faculty in the
   respective centers in the institute, the goal of the curriculum is to
   involve students in an active learning graduate degree that integrates
   the content of these various disciplines. This paper will describe the
   two courses, the curriculum and content in each strand, and the
   implementation of the program to this point.},
